### 2. DATA TYPES - COMPREHENSIVE GUIDE

#### Character Data Types
```sql
-- VARCHAR2: Variable length, efficient storage
CREATE TABLE test_varchar (
    id NUMBER,
    description VARCHAR2(255)  -- 0-4000 bytes
);
-- Key Points:
-- - Only stores used bytes (if 50 chars used in 255 field, stores 50)
-- - More efficient than CHAR
-- - Best for varying length strings

-- CHAR: Fixed length, pads with spaces
CREATE TABLE test_char (
    id NUMBER,
    code CHAR(10)  -- Always 10 characters
);
-- Key Points:
-- - Pads with spaces if content is shorter
-- - Wastes space but faster for fixed-length data
-- - Good for codes or flags

-- NVARCHAR2: Unicode support
CREATE TABLE test_nvarchar (
    id NUMBER,
    name NVARCHAR2(50)
);

-- INSERT vs CHAR length comparison
INSERT INTO test_varchar VALUES (1, 'ABC');      -- Stores 3 bytes
INSERT INTO test_char VALUES (1, 'ABC');         -- Stores 10 bytes (ABC + 7 spaces)
```

#### Numeric Data Types
```sql
-- NUMBER(p,s): precision (total digits), scale (decimals)
CREATE TABLE test_numbers (
    id NUMBER,
    salary NUMBER(10,2),          -- 10 total digits, 2 decimal (99999999.99)
    quantity NUMBER(5),           -- 5 total digits, 0 decimal (99999)
    percentage NUMBER(3,2),       -- 3 total digits, 2 decimal (9.99)
    precise_value NUMBER(38,10)   -- Maximum precision
);

-- Practical examples
INSERT INTO test_numbers VALUES (1, 50000.50, 100, 9.99, 1234567890.1234567890);

-- INTEGER: Whole numbers only
CREATE TABLE test_integer (
    count_value INTEGER
);

Key Points:
- NUMBER is most flexible and default
- Use INTEGER when decimal not needed
- Always specify precision and scale for clarity
- Precision affects storage and performance
```

#### Date & Time Data Types
```sql
-- DATE: Date and time (precision: seconds)
CREATE TABLE test_date (
    id NUMBER,
    event_date DATE DEFAULT SYSDATE
);

-- TIMESTAMP: Higher precision (microseconds)
CREATE TABLE test_timestamp (
    id NUMBER,
    exact_time TIMESTAMP DEFAULT SYSTIMESTAMP,
    with_tz TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE
);

-- Examples
INSERT INTO test_date VALUES (1, SYSDATE);
INSERT INTO test_timestamp VALUES (1, SYSTIMESTAMP);

-- Comparisons
SELECT event_date FROM test_date WHERE event_date > SYSDATE - 7;
SELECT exact_time FROM test_timestamp 
WHERE exact_time > SYSTIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '1' DAY;

Key Points:
- DATE is sufficient for most applications
- TIMESTAMP needed for high-precision timing
- TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE for distributed systems
- Use SYSDATE vs SYSTIMESTAMP appropriately
```

#### LOB (Large Object) Data Types
```sql
-- CLOB: Character Large Object (text up to 4GB)
CREATE TABLE documents (
    doc_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
    content CLOB,
    created_date DATE DEFAULT SYSDATE
);

-- BLOB: Binary Large Object (images, files up to 4GB)
CREATE TABLE media (
    media_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
    file_content BLOB,
    file_type VARCHAR2(50),
    file_size NUMBER
);

-- Working with CLOB
DECLARE
    v_content CLOB;
BEGIN
    DBMS_LOB.createtemporary(v_content, TRUE);
    DBMS_LOB.append(v_content, 'Document content here...');
    INSERT INTO documents (doc_id, content) 
    VALUES (1, v_content);
END;
/

Key Points:
- LOBs are stored separately from table data
- Efficient for large content storage
- Automatic cleanup with TEMPORARY
- Use DBMS_LOB package for manipulation
```

