### 6. SEQUENCES - THEORY & PATTERNS

#### Sequence Fundamentals

```sql
-- Basic sequence creation
CREATE SEQUENCE emp_seq
    START WITH 1000
    INCREMENT BY 1
    MINVALUE 1
    MAXVALUE 99999999
    CYCLE
    CACHE 20;

Theory:
- START WITH: First value (default 1)
- INCREMENT BY: Increment amount (default 1)
- MINVALUE: Minimum value
- MAXVALUE: Maximum value
- CYCLE: Restart from MINVALUE when MAXVALUE reached
- CACHE: Cache size in memory (speeds up access)

-- Using sequences
INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, emp_name)
VALUES (emp_seq.NEXTVAL, 'John Doe');

-- Get current value
SELECT emp_seq.CURRVAL FROM dual;

Key Points:
- NEXTVAL increments and returns value
- CURRVAL returns last used value
- Cannot reference CURRVAL before NEXTVAL
- Sequence independent of table
- Useful for surrogate keys
```

#### Common Sequence Patterns

```sql
-- Pattern 1: ID generation with padding
CREATE SEQUENCE cust_seq START WITH 1000 INCREMENT BY 1;

INSERT INTO customers (cust_id, cust_name)
VALUES ('CUST' || LPAD(cust_seq.NEXTVAL, 6, '0'), 'John');
-- Result: CUST001000

-- Pattern 2: Year-based sequence reset
CREATE SEQUENCE ord_seq_2025
    START WITH 1
    INCREMENT BY 1
    MAXVALUE 999999;

INSERT INTO orders (order_id, order_date)
VALUES ('2025' || LPAD(ord_seq_2025.NEXTVAL, 6, '0'), SYSDATE);
-- Result: 2025000001

-- Pattern 3: Multi-database sequence coordination
-- Store sequence value in control table
CREATE TABLE seq_control (
    seq_name VARCHAR2(50) PRIMARY KEY,
    seq_value NUMBER
);

INSERT INTO seq_control VALUES ('ORD_SEQ', 1000);

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_next_order_id RETURN VARCHAR2 IS
    v_id VARCHAR2(20);
BEGIN
    UPDATE seq_control SET seq_value = seq_value + 1 
    WHERE seq_name = 'ORD_SEQ'
    RETURNING seq_value INTO v_id;
    
    RETURN 'ORD' || v_id;
END get_next_order_id;
/

Key Points:
- Gaps possible (transaction rollback)
- No guarantees of contiguous values
- Cache improves performance
- Cannot disable/enable (drop and recreate if needed)
```

