### 8. SQL JOINS - VISUAL & PRACTICAL

#### Join Types Visualization

```
INNER JOIN: A ∩ B (Common records)
-----------
SELECT * FROM employees e
INNER JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: Only employees with matching departments

LEFT JOIN: A ∪ (A ∩ B) (All from left + matching)
-----------
SELECT * FROM employees e
LEFT JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All employees, departments filled with NULL if not found

RIGHT JOIN: B ∪ (A ∩ B) (All from right + matching)
-----------
SELECT * FROM employees e
RIGHT JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All departments, employees filled with NULL if not found

FULL OUTER JOIN: A ∪ B (All from both)
-----------
SELECT * FROM employees e
FULL OUTER JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All employees and all departments, NULL where no match

CROSS JOIN: A × B (Cartesian product)
-----------
SELECT * FROM employees e
CROSS JOIN departments d;

Result: Every employee paired with every department
```

#### Advanced Join Patterns

```sql
-- Multiple joins
SELECT 
    e.emp_name,
    d.dept_name,
    p.project_name,
    pr.hours_allocated
FROM employees e
JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id
JOIN emp_projects pr ON e.emp_id = pr.emp_id
JOIN projects p ON pr.project_id = p.project_id
WHERE pr.hours_allocated > 20;

-- Self-join (Manager hierarchy)
SELECT 
    e1.emp_name as employee,
    e2.emp_name as manager
FROM employees e1
LEFT JOIN employees e2 ON e1.manager_id = e2.emp_id;

-- Anti-join (Find records not in another table)
SELECT * FROM employees e
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM emp_projects ep 
    WHERE ep.emp_id = e.emp_id
);
-- Employees not assigned to any project

-- Semi-join (Exists but don't repeat)
SELECT DISTINCT e.* FROM employees e
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM emp_projects ep 
    WHERE ep.emp_id = e.emp_id
    AND ep.hours_allocated > 40
);
-- Employees allocated > 40 hours

Key Points:
- JOIN order affects performance
- Index on join columns crucial
- Use EXISTS instead of IN for large datasets
- Explicit joins better than implicit (WHERE clause joins)
```

