### 9. SUBQUERIES - PATTERNS & OPTIMIZATION

#### Subquery Classifications

```sql
-- Scalar subquery (returns single value)
SELECT 
    emp_name, 
    salary,
    (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees) as avg_salary
FROM employees;

Performance: Can return multiple rows, but compared against single value

-- Inline view subquery (returns result set)
SELECT 
    dept_name,
    emp_count
FROM (
    SELECT 
        dept_id,
        COUNT(*) as emp_count
    FROM employees
    GROUP BY dept_id
) dept_emp_count
WHERE emp_count > 5;

Performance: Good for complex aggregations

-- Correlated subquery (references outer query)
SELECT 
    e.emp_name,
    e.salary
FROM employees e
WHERE salary > (
    SELECT AVG(salary)
    FROM employees e2
    WHERE e2.dept_id = e.dept_id
);

Performance: Can be slow (executed for each outer row)

-- Uncorrelated subquery (independent)
SELECT 
    e.emp_name,
    e.salary
FROM employees e
WHERE dept_id IN (
    SELECT dept_id
    FROM departments
    WHERE location = 'New York'
);

Performance: Faster (executed once)
```

#### Subquery Performance Optimization

```sql
-- SLOW: IN with subquery on large dataset
SELECT * FROM orders o
WHERE customer_id IN (
    SELECT customer_id FROM customers 
    WHERE registration_date > '01-JAN-2025'
);

-- FASTER: EXISTS clause
SELECT o.* FROM orders o
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM customers c
    WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id
    AND c.registration_date > '01-JAN-2025'
);

-- FASTEST: JOIN (when possible)
SELECT o.* FROM orders o
JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE c.registration_date > '01-JAN-2025';

Key Points:
- EXISTS is generally faster than IN for large sets
- JOINs often better than subqueries
- Correlate when necessary but be aware of cost
- Use EXPLAIN PLAN to verify optimization
```

