### 10. INDEXING STRATEGY - COMPREHENSIVE

#### When to Create Indexes

```sql
-- DO create indexes:
-- 1. Foreign key columns
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_dept ON employees(dept_id);

-- 2. Columns in WHERE clause
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_status ON employees(status);

-- 3. Join condition columns
CREATE INDEX idx_order_cust ON orders(customer_id);

-- 4. Columns in ORDER BY
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_salary_desc ON employees(salary DESC);

-- 5. Columns with high selectivity
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_email ON employees(email);
-- 100 unique values for 10000 rows = Good selectivity

-- DON'T create indexes:
-- 1. Low selectivity columns
-- CREATE INDEX idx_emp_gender ON employees(gender);
-- Only 2 values (M/F) - full table scan faster

-- 2. Small tables
-- CREATE INDEX idx_small ON small_table(col);
-- < 1000 rows doesn't benefit from index

-- 3. Heavily updated columns
-- CREATE INDEX idx_updated_date ON orders(updated_date);
-- Index maintenance cost > benefit

-- 4. NULL heavy columns
-- CREATE INDEX idx_optional_field ON table(optional_field);
-- NULLs not indexed, reduces effectiveness
```

#### Index Maintenance

```sql
-- Analyze index for statistics
ANALYZE INDEX idx_emp_dept COMPUTE STATISTICS;

-- View index info
SELECT index_name, table_name, column_name, column_position
FROM user_ind_columns
ORDER BY index_name, column_position;

-- Find unused indexes
SELECT index_name, table_name, monitoring
FROM user_indexes
WHERE monitoring = 'YES';

-- Check index fragmentation
SELECT index_name, leaf_blocks, distinct_keys, avg_leaf_blocks_per_key
FROM user_indexes
WHERE table_name = 'EMPLOYEES';

-- Rebuild fragmented index
ALTER INDEX idx_emp_dept REBUILD;

-- Drop unused index
DROP INDEX idx_emp_email;

Key Points:
- Regular analysis maintains performance
- Monitor index usage
- Rebuild when fragmented
- Drop unused indexes (overhead on DML)
```

