**Q6: What is a transaction and transaction control?**

A:
```
Transaction: Logical unit of work with multiple SQL statements

BEGIN TRANSACTION (Implicit)
  SQL 1
  SQL 2
  SQL 3
COMMIT or ROLLBACK (Explicit)

Transaction Control Commands:

1. COMMIT - Makes changes permanent
   BEGIN
       INSERT INTO employees VALUES (1, 'John', 50000);
       UPDATE departments SET budget = budget + 100000;
       COMMIT;  -- Both changes permanent
   END;

2. ROLLBACK - Undo all changes
   BEGIN
       UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100;
       IF error_occurs THEN
           ROLLBACK;  -- Undo everything
       END IF;
   END;

3. SAVEPOINT - Create checkpoint
   BEGIN
       INSERT INTO table1 VALUES (...);
       SAVEPOINT sp1;
       INSERT INTO table2 VALUES (...);  -- Fails
       ROLLBACK TO sp1;  -- Undo table2 only
       COMMIT;  -- table1 committed
   END;

Best Practices:
- Keep transactions short
- Commit frequently to avoid locks
- Use savepoints for complex operations
- Handle exceptions with rollback
```

