**Q11: Explain different SQL JOIN types with examples.**

A:
```
INNER JOIN - Common records only
SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
INNER JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: Only employees with matching departments
Missing: Employees with NULL dept_id, departments with no employees

LEFT JOIN - All from left + matching right
SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
LEFT JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All employees, NULL for departments if not found

RIGHT JOIN - All from right + matching left
SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
RIGHT JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All departments, NULL for employees if not found

FULL OUTER JOIN - All from both
SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
FULL OUTER JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

Result: All employees and all departments, NULL where no match

CROSS JOIN - Cartesian product
SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
CROSS JOIN departments d;

Result: Every employee paired with every department

SELF JOIN - Join table to itself
SELECT e1.emp_name as employee, e2.emp_name as manager
FROM employees e1
LEFT JOIN employees e2 ON e1.manager_id = e2.emp_id;

Result: Employee-manager hierarchy

Performance Tip:
- INNER JOIN fastest (filters rows early)
- LEFT JOIN slightly slower
- FULL OUTER JOIN slowest
- Index join columns for better performance
```

