**Q17: What is MERGE statement and when to use it?**

A:
```
MERGE: Conditional INSERT/UPDATE in single operation

Syntax:
MERGE INTO target_table t
USING source_table s
ON t.key_column = s.key_column
WHEN MATCHED THEN
    UPDATE SET t.col1 = s.col1, t.col2 = s.col2
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (key_column, col1, col2) 
    VALUES (s.key_column, s.col1, s.col2);

Example - Upsert operation:
MERGE INTO employees t
USING emp_staging s
ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id
WHEN MATCHED THEN
    UPDATE SET 
        t.salary = s.salary,
        t.dept_id = s.dept_id
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (emp_id, emp_name, salary, dept_id)
    VALUES (s.emp_id, s.emp_name, s.salary, s.dept_id);

Advantages:
- Atomic operation
- Faster than separate UPDATE and INSERT
- Cleaner code
- Single table scan

When to Use:
- Data warehouse updates
- Synchronizing tables
- ETL operations
- Bulk data loads

DELETE Clause:
MERGE INTO employees t
USING emp_staging s
ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id
WHEN MATCHED AND s.action = 'DELETE' THEN
    DELETE
WHEN MATCHED AND s.action = 'UPDATE' THEN
    UPDATE SET t.salary = s.salary
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (emp_id, emp_name, salary)
    VALUES (s.emp_id, s.emp_name, s.salary);
```

