**Q22: How does OPTIMIZER work and when do you use HINTS?**

A:
```
Query Optimizer: Determines best execution plan

Optimizer Types:

1. Rule-Based Optimizer (Old)
   - Uses predefined rules
   - Mostly deprecated
   - Can be inefficient

2. Cost-Based Optimizer (Modern)
   - Calculates execution cost
   - Uses statistics
   - Generally better decisions

Optimizer Decision Factors:
- Table statistics (rows, blocks)
- Column statistics (distinct values, NULL count)
- Index statistics (leaf blocks, depth)
- Join methods available
- CPU and I/O costs

Hints: Manual optimizer guidance

HINT SYNTAX:
SELECT /*+ HINT_NAME */ column
FROM table;

Common Hints:

1. INDEX hint - Force index usage
   SELECT /*+ INDEX(e idx_emp_name) */
   FROM employees e WHERE emp_name = 'John';

2. FULL hint - Force full table scan
   SELECT /*+ FULL(e) */
   FROM employees e WHERE status = 'ACTIVE';

3. LEADING hint - Join order
   SELECT /*+ LEADING(d) */
   FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

4. USE_HASH hint - Hash join
   SELECT /*+ USE_HASH(e d) */
   FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;

5. PARALLEL hint - Parallel execution
   SELECT /*+ PARALLEL(e 4) */
   FROM employees e WHERE dept_id = 10;

When to Use Hints:
- Optimizer making poor choices
- After analyzing EXPLAIN PLAN
- Performance critical queries
- Temporary fix (better to update statistics)

When NOT to Use Hints:
- Statistics accurate
- Optimizer performing well
- First attempt at optimization

Update Statistics:
EXEC DBMS_STATS.gather_table_stats('HR', 'EMPLOYEES');
EXEC DBMS_STATS.gather_database_stats();

Key Points:
- Good statistics more important than hints
- Hints can become liabilities (version upgrades, data changes)
- Use judiciously
- Document why hint is needed
```

