**Q24: Explain BACKUP & RECOVERY strategies.**

A:
```
Backup Types:

1. Physical Backup (RMAN)
   - Copies database files
   - Complete database recovery possible
   - Faster recovery
   - Production standard
   
   RMAN> BACKUP DATABASE;
   RMAN> BACKUP DATABASE PLUS ARCHIVELOG;

2. Logical Backup (Export/Import)
   - Exports data as SQL
   - Selective restore possible
   - Slower than physical
   - Good for table-level recovery
   
   expdp scott/tiger DIRECTORY=dump_dir DUMPFILE=backup.dmp
   impdp scott/tiger DIRECTORY=dump_dir DUMPFILE=backup.dmp

Backup Strategy:

Full Backup:
RMAN> BACKUP DATABASE;
-- Complete copy of all datafiles
-- Foundation for recovery

Incremental Backup:
RMAN> BACKUP INCREMENTAL LEVEL 0 DATABASE;  -- Full
RMAN> BACKUP INCREMENTAL LEVEL 1 DATABASE;  -- Changes only

Archive Log Backup:
RMAN> BACKUP ARCHIVELOG ALL;
-- Essential for point-in-time recovery

Recovery Scenarios:

Complete Recovery (No data loss):
1. Database lost/corrupted
   RMAN> RESTORE DATABASE;
   RMAN> RECOVER DATABASE;
   ALTER DATABASE OPEN RESETLOGS;

2. Tablespace lost:
   ALTER TABLESPACE users OFFLINE IMMEDIATE;
   RMAN> RESTORE TABLESPACE users;
   RMAN> RECOVER TABLESPACE users;
   ALTER TABLESPACE users ONLINE;

Point-in-Time Recovery:
-- Recover to specific point in time
RMAN> RECOVER DATABASE UNTIL TIME '29-DEC-2025 14:30:00';

Incomplete Recovery:
-- Cannot go beyond missing archive logs
-- Requires RESETLOGS (some data loss)

Best Practices:
1. Regular backups (daily minimum)
2. Test recovery procedures
3. Multiple backup copies
4. Off-site backup copies
5. Archive log retention
6. Document backup location and procedure
7. Monitor backup success
8. Redundant storage (RAID, cloud)

Testing Recovery:
-- Clone to test environment
-- Verify data integrity
-- Document recovery time

3-2-1 Strategy:
- 3 copies of data
- 2 different media
- 1 off-site copy
```

